"Does this lesson promise that brush practice will make your pencil writing better? Why or why not?": "44e63a47ad5b",
"For fourteen lessons you have followed the brush. But on most days you write with a pencil or a pen. Taiwan's curriculum calls this kind of writing 硬筆字, hard-pen writing, because the tip does not bend the way brush hairs do. So here is a fair question for the last lesson: does brush writing help your pencil writing?": "7b3923f5670e",
"For fourteen lessons you have followed the brush. But on most days you write with a pencil or a pen. Taiwan's curriculum calls this kind of writing 硬筆字, hard-pen writing, because the tip does not bend the way brush hairs do. So here is a fair question for the last lesson: does brush writing help your pencil writing? Start with what is different. A brush is soft, so its line swells when you press and thins when you lift. A pencil is hard. Its core is a thin rod of graphite mixed with clay, and it leaves a line of almost the same width from beginning to end. In our model, the brush line in 永 changes from 0.3 to 2.5 centimeters wide. The pencil line stays at 0.3. Now look at what is the same. In the model, a brush and a pencil write 永 at the same moment. Both make five strokes. Both follow the same stroke order. Both travel the same path, and every stroke lands in the same position on the grid. Take the width away from the brush character, and you get the pencil character. It looks like a skeleton. Writers have a name for this skeleton: structure, or 間架結構 in Chinese. It means where each stroke starts and ends, how long it is, and how the strokes balance one another. Taiwan's curriculum asks students in Grades 3 to 6 to learn this structure and to write correct, neat hard-pen characters. It also says that in those grades hard-pen writing comes first, and the brush is the helper. So, does the brush help? Here is an honest answer. We could not find a study that proves brush lessons make pencil writing better, so we will not promise that. What we can say is what the model shows: the two tools share stroke order and structure. A brush character is also big. The curriculum suggests squares of 8 to 12 centimeters for large brush characters, and 1.5 to 2 centimeters for hard-pen writing in Grades 1 and 2, so a stroke in the wrong position is easier to see. Now try it yourself. Pull the slider and watch the thickness disappear. Find the stroke that is off in the game. Then write with both tools on the pad. The curriculum's advice is simple: aim for correct first, then upright, then neat, and beautiful last. It also says not to practice too much. Good writing is a habit, and you can build it with any tool.": "8f25e7a12a2c",
